2. There are two red balls, 4 yellow balls, and 1 blue ball in a box. Two balls are drawn randomly one by one with replacement. Find the probabilities of the following events happening. 
\quad a. Two yellow balls are drawn. 
\quad b. The two balls drawn are not yellow 
\quad c. Only one yellow ball is drawn 
\quad d. At least one ball drawn is not yellow 
\quad e. The two balls drawn are of the same color 
Solutions: 
\quad a. Since the balls are drawn one by one with replacement this is an example of independent events and to get the probability of independent events P(yellow and yellow) = P(yellow) ∙ P(yellow). The probability of getting yellow first is \frac{4}{7} since there were 4 yellow balls and 7 balls in all, the probability of getting yellow for the second time is \frac{4}{7} since replacement was made after getting the first ball, so there were still 4 yellow balls and 7 balls in all. Therefore the P(yellow, yellow) = \frac{4}{7} x \frac{4}{7} = \frac{16}{49}. 
\quad b. There were 3 colors which is not yellow, and 7 colors in all. 
\quad Therefore, 
\quad \begin{align} 
\quad P(not yellow, not yellow) &= P(not yellow) ∙ P(not yellow) 
\quad &= \frac{3}{7} ∙ \frac{3}{7} = \frac{9}{49} 
\quad \end{align} 
\quad c. If only yellow ball is drawn, then we can have P(yellow, not yellow) being yellow first or P(not yellow, yellow) being not yellow first, so we will have 
\quad Therefore, 
\quad \begin{align} 
\quad P(yellow, not yellow) + P(not yellow, yellow) &= \frac{4}{7} ∙ \frac{3}{7} + \frac{3}{7} ∙ \frac{4}{7}  
\quad &= \frac{12}{49} + \frac{12}{49} 
\quad &= \frac{24}{49} 
\quad \end{align} 
\quad d. At least one ball drawn is yellow means P(yellow, not yellow) or P(not yellow, yellow) having one yellow or P(yellow, yellow) having two yellows. So we will have it equal to; 
\quad \begin{align} 
\quad &= \frac{4}{7} ∙ \frac{3}{7} + \frac{3}{7} ∙ \frac{4}{7} + \frac{4}{7} ∙ \frac{4}{7}  
\quad &= \frac{12}{49} + \frac{12}{49} + \frac{16}{49}  
\quad &= \frac{40}{49} 
\quad \end{align} 
\quad Another solution is the use of complement. The complement of at least one ball drawn is yellow is getting no yellow ball. So P(not yellow, not yellow) = \frac{3}{7} ∙ \frac{3}{7} = \frac{9}{49} , it is the complement of getting at least one yellow ball we need to subtract it form 1. So we will have; 
\quad \begin{align} 
\quad &= 1 - \frac{9}{49}
\quad &= \frac{40}{49} 
\quad \end{align} 
\quad which the same as the first answer. We can always use the complement if the word at least one was used in the problem. 
\quad c. If two balls drawn are of the same color we will have P(yellow, yellow) or P(red, red) we can't get same blue since there is only one blue. Hence we will have it 
\quad \begin{align} 
\quad &= \frac{4}{7} ∙ \frac{4}{7} + \frac{2}{7} ∙ \frac{2}{7} 
\quad &= \frac{16}{49} + \frac{4}{49} 
\quad &= \frac{20}{49} 
\quad \end{align}

3. Three cards are chosen at random from a deck of 52 cards without replacement. What is the probability of choosing a king, a queen, and an ace in order?  
Solution: 
Since this is choosing a card without replacement and in order also means one at a time, this is an example of dependent events. To get the probability of dependent events in this example; 
P(king, queen, ace) = P(king) ∙ P(queen after king) ∙ P(ace after queen) 
First let us solve the following; 
P(King)= \frac{4}{52} since there were 4 kings and 52 cards in all  
P(queen after king) = \frac{4}{51} since there were 4 queens and only 51 cards left after getting the first card which is the king. 
P(ace after the queen) = \frac{4}{50} since there were 4 aces and only 50 cards were left after getting the king as the first card and the queen as the second card.
Going back to the formula, substitute each value, we will have; 
\begin{align} 
P(king, queen, ace) &= P(king) ∙ P(queen after king) ∙ P(ace after queen) 
&= \frac{4}{52} ∙ \frac{4}{51} ∙ \frac{4}{50}  
&= \frac{4^3}{P(52, 3)} ∙ \frac{4}{51} ∙ \frac{4}{50} 
&= \frac{64}{132600}  
&= \frac{8}{16575}  
\end{align} 
What if order is not necessary? How will you solve for it? If order is not necessary it also means you will get the item at the same time. That will involve combination.
